        "content": [
          "Latin has a shape for giving an order, and",
          "it is the shortest form a verb owns: the",
          "stem with its family vowel, nothing after",
          "it.",
          "",
          "   -āre verbs:  vocā!  = call!",
          "   -ēre verbs:  tenē!  = hold!",
          "",
          "   Tenē viam, mīles, et castra pete!",
          "   = Keep to the road, soldier, and make",
          "     for the camp!",
          "",
          "Speaking to more than one person adds -te:",
          "",
          "   vocāte!   tenēte!",
          "",
          "Recognition only: knowing an order when one",
          "lands, never issuing one."
        ]
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "Two shapes sit close enough to confuse:",
          "   vocat  = he calls    (a statement)",
          "   vocā   = call!       (an order)",
          "",
          "The order carries no personal ending, and",
          "that missing -t is the whole difference.",
          "The plural pair runs the same way:",
          "",
          "   vocātis = you all call",
          "   vocāte  = call, all of you!",
          "",
          "Verbs outside these two families end the",
          "singular order in -e or -ī:",
          "   Pugnā et oppidum dēfende!",
          "   = Fight and defend the town!",
          "",
          "Four cut it shorter still: dīc (say),",
          "dūc (lead), fac (do), fer (bring)."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
